<Layout
	title="Reading"
	description="Books, speeches, and ideas that shaped how we think"
	pubDate={new Date()}
>
	<p>
		The ideas that matter don't come from productivity blogs. They come from people who saw 
		things others couldn't—or wouldn't—see. This is what we're reading, watching, and thinking about.
	</p>

	<hr />

	<h2>📚 Books</h2>

	<h3>The Sovereign Individual (1997)</h3>
	<p><em>James Dale Davidson & Lord William Rees-Mogg</em></p>
	<p>
		Written in 1997, this book predicted the rise of digital money, remote work, and the decline 
		of nation-state power with eerie accuracy. The core thesis: technology empowers individuals 
		over institutions. The capable will thrive; the dependent will struggle.
	</p>
	<blockquote>
		"The technology of the Information Age makes it possible for productive people to 
		escape the tyranny of place."
	</blockquote>
	<p><a href="/blog/this-is-water-sovereign-individual">Read our thoughts →</a></p>

	<hr />

	<h2>🎤 Speeches</h2>

	<h3>This Is Water (2005)</h3>
	<p><em>David Foster Wallace — Kenyon College Commencement</em></p>
	<p>
		The most important realities are the hardest to see because they're everywhere. 
		Wallace's speech is about attention, awareness, and the choice of what to think about. 
		Twenty years later, it's more relevant than ever.
	</p>
	<blockquote>
		"There are these two young fish swimming along and they happen to meet an older fish 
		swimming the other way, who nods at them and says 'Morning, boys. How's the water?' 
		And the two young fish swim on for a bit, and then eventually one of them looks over 
		at the other and goes 'What the hell is water?'"
	</blockquote>
	<p><a href="https://fs.blog/david-foster-wallace-this-is-water/" target="_blank">Read the full speech →</a></p>

	<hr />

	<h2>More Coming</h2>
